Z||000006||sid1200||02-20-2000||05:10 PM||||Speaking of paint, anyone get their car painted at the "new" Scheib.  they are promoting like crazy their top of the line Level 3 jobs, their web site looks good, but I am suspicious , any one have anything good/bad to say.<P>thanks||||reg||11
Z||000007||stimpy||02-20-2000||06:45 PM||||I have heard a lot more bad feedback about Earl Scheib than Maaco.  I would do as wkayl is doing and do the wet-sand on it after it has had sufficient time to cure.  But if you can't get satisfaction, then I would take advantage of that warranty.<BR>BTW the most negative feedback about Maaco that I have heard is that they can't mask worth a darn. I am pondering taking my car apart next winter and having them paint it in peices. As long as the color matches from piece to piece, it should be alright.  Any comments from the older and wiser element?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||02-20-2000||07:06 PM||||Stimpy... Based on very limited research, I would tend to agree with you. I saw a Maaco job that the car's owner was about to drive home. This was the *second* car that he had had painted there, so I suppose he was pretty happy with the work. The paint was gorgeous, but there was a small amount of paint on the black windshield molding, etc. I will probably let them paint my car, but I plan to remove all of the trim that I can, and mask all of the difficult stuff, myself. I'm going to try to negotiate a free upgrade to BC/CC, since I'm doing so much of the work myself. <BR>BTW... Anybody know anything about flex agents for the front and rear fascia? I'm told that a lot of folks don't use it. If I get *tapped*, and my paint crumbles, I'm gonna be an unhappy camper.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||02-20-2000||07:29 PM||||I've heard that, if you leave the original paint on and just paint over top of it, you don't have to add a flex agent, but I don't really know anything about paint.||||reg||1
